>Is there a
>good way to keep my hungry dogs away from the corpses?

 Plenty.  Lock them in another room.  Block them out with boulder(s).
(You'll eventually end up with a number of boulders.)  Spell of wizard
lock or wand of locking can help.  Or leave them on another level to go
feral & retame when you want them.

  Sacrifest is the best part of playing a wizard.  Almost no other
character can do it.  You essentially take a time out to do safer and
more interesting things than dungeon dive.  You get more spellbooks with
plenty of time/mana to practice them in a safe area.  Not just practice
them to improve spell skill, but research and play with them to see what
they really do.

  Without the research and practical experience you're playing a
crippled wizard no matter how much mana and spells you have.

  Sacrifest, buc testing of equipment, trying on non-cursed armor for AC
& effect on spell casting, more wands, rings, potions, scrolls,
spellbooks (divine & dropped).  Dalliance with foocubi.  A little light
polypiling.  Improving Hp and protection with #praying.  Nurse dancing
on Elbereth squares 

Have a stash in a closet you've dug in a handy location and put a door
on with spell of wizard lock.  Engrave a permanent Elbereth under your
stash with wand of fire/lightning.  Put a cursed object on top to keep
pets away.   Practicing spells on various monsters, etc.  Cast spell of
light every where for practice and convenience.  

  Keeping spell of Detect Monster pumped up so you can see every monster
on the whole level at all times.  

  My favorite experience is dancing with a nurse and two succubi at the
same time.  Standing on Elbereth, the Eye for MR, #chatting to the
succubi to initiate encounters, wearing an amulet of reflection JIC.
Watching approaching succubi with skilled+ Detect Monsters.  Zapping 
spell of knock to unlock the door to let in the succubi, then zapping
wizard lock to slam the door in the face of the monster behind them.
Zapping headachy succubi away with spell of teleport away to let them
recover.  Naming nurses and succubi to help keep track of things.
Sacrificing the odd popcorn monster that shows up without bothering
to put on armor or wield a weapon.  (Magic missile works great.  MR from
the Eye will protect you from bounces.)

  For me, all that control and action at a distance is what being a
wizard is all about.


 Hint: strike peaceful succubi with your bare hand to anger them.  Then
they'll keep returning to you after a successful encounter.  Once you
hit XL 30, keep going.  It's the Hp and Mana you get after you hit XL 30
that really start to help.

  Learn to kill monsters on the altar for ease of sacrificing and buc
identification.  Get a chest/box just for junk.


  It can be a nice, safe, interesting, educational (in more ways than
one), character developing 20,000+ turns.  You can even let non-critical
spells expire so you can synchronize them.

  Instead of the dungeon controlling you, you control the dungeon.  It's
a pleasant experience.

  When you finish with a fully developed wizard with plenty of spells
and, most importantly, knowing how and when to use them, Gehennom will
be a totally different experience.

